        "content": "{\n  \"status\": \"partial\",\n  \"material_usefulness\": \"supporting\",\n  \"useful_material\": \"This prospective cohort study (n=52) measured quantitative concentrations of short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs) in human plasma and fecal samples from patients with solid tumors treated with PD-1 inhibitors. The authors reported median concentrations in μmol/g for multiple SCFAs, linking them to immune checkpoint inhibitor outcomes. Specifically, fecal propionic acid concentrations were significantly higher in responders (median 172.6 μmol/g) compared to nonresponders (median 103.6 μmol/g), and high fecal propionic acid was independently associated with longer progression-free survival (multivariate HR, 0.07; 95% CI, 0.03-0.19). Plasma isovaleric acid was also significantly higher in responders (median 0.3 μmol/g) vs nonresponders (median 0.0 μmol/g) and associated with progression-free survival (HR, 0.38; 95% CI, 0.14-0.99). Other measured SCFAs included acetic, butyric, valeric, isobutyric, hydroangelic, caproic, and succinic acids in both matrices. This provides quantitative metabolite data in a systemic biofluid (plasma) correlated with ICI efficacy, addressing the 'serum' and 'quantitative data' aspects of the success criteria for SCFAs.\",\n  \"auxiliary_material\": \"The study design was a prospective cohort biomarker study conducted at Kyoto University Hospital (2016-2019). Samples were collected before PD-1 inhibitor administration (nivolumab or pembrolizumab). SCFA analysis used ultra-high-performance liquid chromatography coupled with tandem mass spectrometry (UPLC-MS/MS). The authors noted limitations including small sample size, lack of a validation cohort, and that dietary information was retrospective (1 year preceding cancer onset). Mechanistically, the authors discussed SCFAs as potential histone deacetylase inhibitors and immunomodulators but did not measure these mechanisms directly in the tumour microenvironment. The study did not analyze the gut microbiome species composition, focusing solely on metabolite output.\",\n  \"remaining_gap\": \"The supplied material does not provide data on inosine concentrations, which is a specific requirement of the gap question. Furthermore, while the success criteria allow for serum data, the gap question prioritizes concentrations 'inside the human tumour microenvironment'; this study measured peripheral plasma and fecal concentrations, not intratumoral tissue concentrations. Therefore, the physiological levels of SCFAs and inosine specifically within the tumour tissue remain unaddressed. The success criterion for quantitative data of specific metabolites is partially met for SCFAs in plasma but unmet for inosine and tumour tissue.\",\n  \"outline_action\": \"Search for metabolomics studies that specifically profile intratumoral tissue samples from ICI-treated patients to capture TME-specific concentrations. Conduct a targeted search for 'inosine' combined with 'tumour microenvironment' and 'immune checkpoint inhibitor' to find if inosine has been quantified in human tumour tissue or serum in this context. Consider reviewing metabolomic profiling papers that cover broader panels than SCFAs to check for inosine inclusion.\"\n}",
